{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The argument fails because it assumes that preferring a better-corroborated theory logically entails an assumption about the future (induction). However, Popper explicitly distinguishes between the logical status of corroboration (which says nothing about the future) and the pragmatic decision to act on the best-tested theory. For Popper, the preference is not based on the expectation that the theory is true or will remain successful (which would be induction), but is a non-justificational, critical choice: since we cannot know the future, the only rational course is to rely on the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification so far. The target's claim that this 'smuggles in' induction ignores Popper's core contention that one can act rationally without any inductive premise, treating the preference as a methodological rule rather than a probabilistic prediction. By framing the preference as necessarily assuming the uniformity of nature, the target attacks a straw man of Popper's position rather than engaging with his actual distinction between logical appraisal and pragmatic adoption.","counterexample":["A scientist chooses the theory that survived a severe test yesterday not because they believe it will work tomorrow (inductive prediction), but because it is the only theory that has not yet been proven false, making it the most rational basis for action in the absence of certainty."],"premise":"The target conflates Popper's concept of 'corroboration' (a logical appraisal of past performance) with the psychological or pragmatic 'preference' to act, failing to address Popper's specific solution that rational action requires no inductive bridge.","successor_question":"If corroboration provides no probability of future success, on what grounds does Popper claim the preference for the better-corroborated theory is 'rational' rather than merely arbitrary?","target":"a15f4b263b9598e290bb50f8612ea5e31dd02988e16e61a29a969e35df8d58d4"}]}